{
  "dom_id": "questionExplanation-48743",
  "name": null,
  "problem": "",
  "worked_solution": "First, we factor the numerator and denominator and cancel any common factors:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{x}^{2}+5x+6}{kx+2k} & =\\frac{(x+3)(x+2)}{k(x+2)} \\\\  & =\\frac{(x+3)\\cancel{(x+2)}}{k\\cancel{(x+2)}} \\\\  & =\\frac{x+3}{k}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe then can compute the limit at $x=-2,$ and we get\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to -2}g(x)={lim}_{x\\to -2}(\\frac{x+3}{k})=\\frac{1}{k}.\n$$\n\nFor $g(x)$ to be continuous at $x=-2,$ we must have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}g(-2) & ={lim}_{x\\to -2}g(x) \\\\ k & =\\frac{1}{k} \\\\ {k}^{2} & =1 \\\\ k & =\\pm 1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, for the function to be continuous at $x=-2,$ we need $k=\\pm 1.$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
  "fields": [],
  "problem_field_keys": [],
  "answer_fields_complete": null,
  "result": null,
  "proof_feedback": null,
